# {{ .Name }}

A [Fabrin](https://github.com/usefabrin/fabrin) application.

```bash
just run                 # serve on :8080
curl localhost:8080/
curl localhost:8080/healthz   # liveness — consults nothing
curl localhost:8080/readyz    # readiness — aggregates module checks, fails closed
```

## What this binary can do

```bash
go run . help
go run . routes          # every mounted route, and the module that owns it
go run . version
```

No arguments means *serve*.

## Modules

A module owns its routes and, optionally, its health checks, commands, models,
and lifecycle. `home/` is the one this project started with; add more with
`fabrin startapp <name>` and wire them in `main.go`.

**A module never imports another module.** If `orders` needs something `billing`
owns, `orders` declares the interface it needs in its own package and `main.go`
passes in whatever satisfies it. That interface is the seam along which the
module can later move into its own process.

## Deployment shapes

```bash
FABRIN_MODULES=home go run .    # mount only these modules
```

One binary, N deployment shapes. An unknown module name is a startup error, never
a silent no-op.

## Settings

Resolved as defaults ← `.env` ← environment ← flags, later layers winning.

| Key | Default | |
|---|---|---|
| `FABRIN_ADDR` | `:8080` | listen address |
| `FABRIN_MODULES` | *(all)* | which modules this process mounts |
| `FABRIN_LOG_FORMAT` | `json` | `json` or `text` |
| `FABRIN_LOG_LEVEL` | `info` | |
| `FABRIN_DEBUG` | `false` | development conveniences; leave off in production |

Every one also works as a flag: `go run . -addr :9000`.
